592186350848 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350848 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350848 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350848 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350848 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350848 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350848 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350848 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186350230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350848 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "531e3f0a-9d1a-559d-b818-9180e86b8970" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350878 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350878 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350878 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186349408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350878 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "681dade9-90cc-55fd-9472-6d4a71b243f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351425 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351425 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351425 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351425 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}.$ The matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & b & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & c \\\\ a & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}+2{b}_{3}, \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & ={b}_{1}+3{b}_{3}, \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & ={b}_{2}-{b}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351425 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb9f2bef-7315-5e32-9d14-53800608bfb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351425 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the coordinates of the images relative to the basis $B:$\n\n$$\n[T({b}_{1})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{2})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[T({b}_{3})]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, the $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $b=1,$ $c=1$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=2+1+1=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351429 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351429 #edb/ref 1069 "q-82493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351429 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351429 #edb/ref 1071 "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & c \\\\ a & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & b & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351429 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "696efd0b-1adb-568a-a58e-00ad82c71a3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351429 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 12 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 12 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 12 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $b=0,$ $c=1$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-1)+0+1=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416365 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416365 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416365 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416365 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416365 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 1759218